Subject: Ask for a timing policy before retiming an existing project

Freecut MIT adaptation (FL-94). Matching a project to its first video,
or any other frame-rate change, used to rewrite the rate while every
clip, keyframe and marker kept its frame number, so an existing edit
silently played faster or slower. When the main timeline has content,
the person now chooses:

- keep-time: timeline frames move to the new rate exactly (integer
  ratio, nearest frame, clip ends rounded with their starts, so butted
  clips stay butted). A clip that rounding would overlap is pushed later
  by the overflow, with the clips butted after it, its linked clips and
  the clips at the same frame on the other sync-locked (and unlocked)
  tracks (unless
  that chain leads back to the clip it overlaps; then only clips from
  its frame on move). A clip
  whose rounded length reads a fraction of a frame more of its source
  keeps its length and grows its window, within the media (before its
  start for a reversed clip). The last
  keyframe of a lane keeps the settled value. Media that read their
  source at the timeline rate are pinned to the old rate so they show
  the same pictures.
- keep-frames: the previous behaviour, now an explicit choice.

commitProjectMetadataChange refuses a rate change on content without a
policy, so every caller is covered. The new rate and the retimed
timeline are stored in one project write, and undo or redo of a rate
change stores the restored timeline with its rate, so a reload never
pairs a rate with frames written for another. The dialog re-checks for
content when it is submitted. A timeline drop reads its frame before
the prompt: the frame now keeps its second under keep-time, and a drop
whose timeline the prompt switched away from is abandoned rather than
placed on another timeline's track.

Every project write now runs one at a time, and a timeline save that
captured the stores before a newer timeline write skips its write and
leaves the editor dirty, so an in-flight autosave cannot put back the
older timeline. The project store no longer keeps or writes back a copy
of the timeline: its updates write only the fields they change, and a
late write result never replaces an undo or newer update made
meanwhile. An undo whose write fails says so, leaves the editor unsaved,
and the next timeline save stores those settings with the timeline. Freecut's loadTimeline migration write and root-folder writes
still bypass the write queue; neither stores a timeline captured before
a retime.

Loading a project also kept removing empty trailing audio tracks that
the person had soloed, muted, locked, hidden or taken out of sync lock,
which silently changed the mix after a reload; only tracks with default
controls are tidied now.

Colour needs no prompt: the working range follows the media (FL-97
owner decision, patch 0033).

